# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 has "chain" and "chain → ¬◇infinite" and "¬◇infinite"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 have "chain"; they differ when only "in efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate" and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "in efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate" and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" are true and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" is false. Is S4, "Now in efficient causes it is not possible to go on to infinity, because in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause, whether the intermediate cause be several, or only one.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 have "chain → (¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ (¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "in efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate", "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate cause (ultimate effect)" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2, to: Suppose "in efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate", "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate cause (ultimate effect)" are true and "there is an intermediate efficient cause" is false. Is S5, "Now to take away the cause is to take away the effect.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 have nothing from it; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 has "¬first → ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate"; they differ when only "there is an intermediate efficient cause" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is an intermediate efficient cause" is true and "there is a first efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate cause (ultimate effect)" are false. Is S6, "Therefore, if there be no first cause among efficient causes, there will be no ultimate, nor any intermediate cause.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 have "infinite → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 has "infinite → ¬first" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; they differ when only "in efficient causes one goes on to infinity", "there is an intermediate efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate cause (ultimate effect)" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "in efficient causes one goes on to infinity", "there is an intermediate efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate cause (ultimate effect)" are true and "there is a first efficient cause" is false. Is S7, "But if in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity, there will be no first efficient cause, neither will there be an ultimate effect, nor any intermediate efficient causes; all of which is plainly false.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 have "first → god"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 has "god"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause" and "the first efficient cause is what everyone names God" are false. Is S8, "Therefore it is necessary to admit a first efficient cause, to which everyone gives the name of God.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 have "first"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 has "first ∧ god"; they differ when only "there is a first efficient cause" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause" is true and "the first efficient cause is what everyone names God" is false. Is the conclusion, "Therefore it is necessary to admit a first efficient cause, to which everyone gives the name of God.", true then?
